@* A section is the right unit for this: the wrapper is relative, the layer fills it and falls
   behind the copy, and nothing about the copy's own layout changes. The headings are plain
   text — that is the part a screen reader gets, and the colour is the part it does not. *@
<section class="relative isolate overflow-hidden rounded-lg border px-6 py-10">
    <AuroraBackground />

    <div class="relative mx-auto max-w-prose space-y-3">
        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold">Quiet by default</h3>
        <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            The field is decoration, so the content above never depends on it. Delete the
            <code>AuroraBackground</code> and this section still reads, still navigates, still
            makes sense to a screen reader.
        </p>
    </div>
</section>
